Absolutely. Save this one. This is the cleaned, corrected 3A baseline—not the earlier drafts.

CNS — LOG 3A

FOUNDATION & RUNTIME FABRIC

Engineering Construction Baseline V1.0

Status: ENGINEERING BASELINE — REVIEWED
Relationship: Built from Log 1 Constitution + Log 2 Capability & Intelligence Construction
Purpose: Define the resilient runtime foundation on which the complete CNS ecosystem will be constructed.


---

3A.1 — Construction Objective

The CNS foundation must support a system that can:

coordinate many heterogeneous resources;

survive individual component failures;

replace components without redesigning CNS;

preserve important execution history;

recover interrupted work;

maintain provenance and evidence;

learn from outcomes;

discover capability gaps;

safely expand into new domains;

protect critical financial operations;

continuously evolve without becoming structurally unstable.


The foundation therefore optimizes for:

RELIABILITY
+ REPLACEABILITY
+ OBSERVABILITY
+ RECOVERABILITY
+ TRACEABILITY
+ EXTENSIBILITY

Fundamental rule

> CNS must be modular without becoming fragile. A replaceable component must never become a single uncontrolled point of failure for the ecosystem.




---

3A.2 — CNS Runtime as the Backbone

The CNS Runtime is the common execution fabric.

CNS
                          │
                 ┌────────▼────────┐
                 │  RUNTIME FABRIC │
                 └────────┬────────┘
                          │
       ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐
       ▼                  ▼                  ▼
     STATE              EVENTS             CONTROL
       │                  │                  │
       └──────────────────┼──────────────────┘
                          │
                     ORCHESTRATION
                          │
                        INTENT
                          │
                         WORK
                          │
                    CAPABILITY
                          │
                        METHOD
                          │
                       RESOURCE
                          │
                      EXECUTION
                          │
                    VERIFICATION
                          │
                       ACTION
                          │
                       OUTCOME
                          │
                 STATE + EXPERIENCE
                          │
                  ECOSYSTEM LEARNING

The Runtime is not the financial brain.

It provides the machinery through which the brain, workers, models, engines, memory and execution systems operate.


---

3A.3 — Four Fundamental Objects

CNS explicitly separates:

Intent

What are we trying to achieve?

Work

What must be done?

Capability

What ability is required?

Resource

What actual component can provide that ability?

Therefore:

INTENT
  ↓
WORK
  ↓
REQUIRED CAPABILITIES
  ↓
METHODS
  ↓
AVAILABLE RESOURCES
  ↓
EXECUTION

This separation prevents the architecture from becoming tied to any particular agent, model or technology.


---

3A.4 — Intent Model

Intent represents the purpose and decision context behind work.

Intent {

    intent_id

    goal
    objective
    decision_context

    constraints[]

    authority_required
    consequence_level

    time_horizon
    deadline

    evidence_requirements[]

    success_criteria[]
    stop_conditions[]

    assumptions[]

    uncertainty_tolerance

    provenance
}

Example:

Goal:
Evaluate Company X.

Objective:
Determine whether allocating capital improves expected
risk-adjusted portfolio outcome.

Constraints:
Capital, horizon, liquidity, portfolio exposure.

Evidence:
Financials, business, management, industry,
valuation, competitors, risks, contradictions.

Stop:
Evidence is sufficient OR additional investigation
has insufficient expected value.

Intent is not itself a decision.

It defines what the system is trying to accomplish.


---

3A.5 — Work Model

Intent produces one or more WorkItems.

WorkItem {

    work_id
    intent_id
    parent_id

    goal
    task_type

    required_capabilities[]
    candidate_methods[]

    inputs[]
    context_refs[]

    dependencies[]

    authority

    deadline
    cost_budget
    latency_budget

    completion_criteria[]
    stop_conditions[]

    assigned_resource

    evidence_refs[]
    output_refs[]

    status
    health
    failure_state

    provenance
}

A WorkItem may become a dependency graph when decomposition provides measurable benefit.

Decomposition is not automatic.


---

3A.6 — Capability Model

A Capability describes an ability available to CNS.

Capability {

    capability_id
    version

    purpose

    input_schema
    output_schema

    preconditions[]
    postconditions[]

    authority_required

    quality_metrics[]
    reliability_metrics[]

    latency_profile
    cost_profile

    failure_modes[]

    verification_method

    fallback

    security_policy
}

Examples:

financial_analysis
market_regime_detection
fraud_detection
forecasting
backtesting
portfolio_optimization
evidence_verification
order_validation
market_data_retrieval

A capability does not prescribe its implementation.


---

3A.7 — Method Model

A Capability may be implemented through multiple methods.

Capability
     ↓
Method
     ↓
Resource

Example:

financial_analysis
       ↓
cash-flow consistency analysis
       ↓
financial analysis engine

or:

financial_analysis
       ↓
forensic investigation
       ↓
research agent + tools + model

MethodDescriptor {

    method_id
    capability_id

    procedure

    assumptions[]

    required_inputs[]
    expected_outputs[]

    evidence_requirements[]

    suitable_conditions[]
    unsuitable_conditions[]

    computational_cost

    historical_performance

    failure_modes[]
}

This gives CNS a future mechanism for learning:

> which method works best under which conditions.




---

3A.8 — Resource Model

A Resource is an actual executable component.

ResourceDescriptor {

    resource_id
    resource_type
    version

    capabilities[]
    methods[]

    input_schema
    output_schema

    quality
    reliability

    latency
    cost

    health
    availability

    privacy_class

    hardware_requirements

    authority_scope

    failure_modes[]
}

Resources can include:

4 trading/investment bots
7 ML systems
LLMs
local models
remote models
agents
sub-agents
research engines
backtest engines
risk engines
data providers
execution systems
deterministic functions
human approval

The Runtime does not depend on their internal implementation.


---

3A.9 — Module Boundary

Major CNS capabilities operate behind explicit module boundaries.

Module {

    module_id
    version

    provides[]
    requires[]

    capabilities[]

    dependencies[]

    configuration_schema

    authority_scope

    lifecycle

    health

    compatibility

    failure_boundary

    observability
}

A module may be:

registered
activated
paused
degraded
replaced
retired

without changing the CNS identity.


---

3A.10 — Modularity and Resilience Invariant

> Modularity must increase replaceability without decreasing system integrity.



Every module requires:

explicit interface
dependency declaration
authority boundary
health state
timeout
resource limit
cancellation
failure handling
recovery strategy
version
compatibility
observability
safe shutdown

A module cannot disappear while CNS continues assuming that its capability is healthy.


---

3A.11 — Failure Isolation

Failures are contained at the lowest meaningful level.

ML failure
   ↓
ML resource degraded

not:

ML failure
   ↓
research failure
   ↓
CNS failure

Similarly:

agent failure
→ affected task

task failure
→ affected work

work failure
→ affected intent

Failure propagates upward only when a higher-level invariant is genuinely compromised.


---

3A.12 — Capability-Aware Degradation

CNS does not blindly keep operating when a critical capability disappears.

FAILURE
   ↓
IDENTIFY LOST CAPABILITY
   ↓
IS IT REQUIRED?
   ├── NO → continue degraded
   └── YES
        ↓
     fallback?
      ├── YES → switch
      └── NO → stop affected operation

Example:

Research model unavailable
→ alternate model selected

but:

Risk engine unavailable
→ live trading blocked


---

3A.13 — Event Fabric

Important CNS state transitions are represented by typed events.

CnsEvent {

    event_id
    event_type
    event_version

    timestamp
    sequence

    ecosystem_id

    intent_id
    work_id
    task_id
    resource_id

    parent_event_id
    causation_id
    correlation_id

    actor
    authority

    payload

    input_refs[]
    output_refs[]

    provenance

    state_before_ref
    state_after_ref

    security_class
}

Events record what happened.

They are not themselves current state.


---

3A.14 — Durable and Live Event Planes

CNS explicitly separates event durability.

Durable events

Must survive restart:

decisions
orders
risk events
security events
configuration changes
major failures
important evidence
learning outcomes
system changes

Operational durable events

Retained according to policy:

work transitions
task transitions
resource health
model calls
research activity

Live/ephemeral events

Normally not permanently stored:

token streaming
UI updates
heartbeats
temporary intermediate signals

This prevents the event system from becoming an uncontrolled storage dump.


---

3A.15 — State Model

State answers:

> What is true now?



State is derived and maintained through controlled projections.

EVENT
  ↓
VALIDATE
  ↓
STATE PROJECTOR
  ↓
CURRENT STATE

Example:

resource/degraded
        ↓
ResourceState
health = DEGRADED
availability = false

Historical events remain immutable.


---

3A.16 — State Ownership

Each important state has one authoritative owner.

Bot health
→ Resource subsystem

Task status
→ Work subsystem

Portfolio exposure
→ Portfolio subsystem

Order status
→ Execution subsystem

Model availability
→ Model subsystem

Long-term knowledge
→ Memory subsystem

Other subsystems may read state but cannot silently mutate another subsystem's authoritative state.


---

3A.17 — Source-of-Truth Classification

Every important state value identifies its truth source.

CNS-owned
External authoritative
Derived projection
Cached observation
Estimated/inferred

Examples:

Broker order status
→ external authoritative

CNS task status
→ CNS authoritative

Portfolio risk estimate
→ CNS-derived

Market quote
→ selected market-data source

Cached company information
→ cached observation

This prevents hidden synchronization assumptions.


---

3A.18 — Persistence Boundary

The Runtime does not directly depend on a specific storage technology.

CNS Runtime
      ↓
Persistence Interface
      ↓
Storage Implementation

The implementation may later use different databases, event stores or storage technologies without changing the Runtime contract.

Persistence must support, where required:

durable events
state snapshots/checkpoints
provenance
recovery
replay
versioning


---

3A.19 — Command vs Event

CNS distinguishes:

COMMAND
"What should happen?"

EVENT
"What actually happened?"

Example:

EXECUTE_ORDER

does not mean:

ORDER_EXECUTED

because execution may fail.

Correct flow:

COMMAND
 ↓
AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
VALIDATION
 ↓
EXECUTION
 ↓
SUCCESS → EVENT
FAILURE → FAILURE EVENT


---

3A.20 — Idempotency

Every consequential external command receives a unique idempotency key.

CommandRecord {

    command_id
    idempotency_key

    command_type
    target_resource

    parameters_hash

    status

    result_ref

    created_at
    completed_at
}

If CNS loses a response and retries:

same idempotency key
        ↓
execution history checked
        ↓
already executed?
 ├── YES → reconcile / return known result
 └── NO → execute

CNS does not assume external systems provide exactly-once execution.

Instead it uses:

unique command identity
+
idempotency
+
durable execution records
+
external reconciliation


---

3A.21 — Reconciliation

External reality may differ from CNS's last known state.

Therefore:

LOCAL CNS STATE
       ↕
EXTERNAL SYSTEM
       ↓
RECONCILIATION
       ↓
CORRECTED CNS STATE

Examples:

broker order status
cloud resource status
market-data provider status
payment/external transaction status

After uncertainty or restart, CNS verifies reality instead of assuming.


---

3A.22 — Consistency and Transaction Boundaries

CNS does not impose one consistency model on the entire ecosystem.

Each subsystem defines whether a transition requires:

atomicity
ordering
eventual consistency
reconciliation

Critical financial operations receive stricter guarantees.

Example:

ORDER
 ↓
EXTERNAL EXECUTION RESULT
 ↓
EXECUTION EVENT
 ↓
PORTFOLIO UPDATE
 ↓
RISK STATE UPDATE

The implementation must define the consistency boundary for this sequence.


---

3A.23 — Work State Machine

CREATED
   ↓
READY
   ↓
ASSIGNED
   ↓
RUNNING
   │
   ├── WAITING
   ├── BLOCKED
   ├── PAUSED
   ├── FAILED
   │     ↓
   │  RETRY / REASSIGN / REPLAN
   │
   └── COMPLETED

Terminal states:

COMPLETED
FAILED_FINAL
CANCELLED
EXPIRED

No task can silently become completed without completion evidence.


---

3A.24 — Work Lease

A resource receives temporary ownership of execution.

Lease {

    lease_id
    work_id
    task_id
    resource_id

    issued_at
    expires_at

    attempt
}

If the worker disappears:

lease expires
 ↓
task becomes recoverable
 ↓
CNS decides:
retry
reassign
replan
cancel


---

3A.25 — Checkpoint and Resume

Long-running work can be checkpointed.

Checkpoint {

    checkpoint_id
    work_id

    task_states[]
    completed_tasks[]
    active_tasks[]
    pending_tasks[]

    outputs[]
    evidence_refs[]

    resource_assignments[]

    decision_context

    schema_version
    created_at
}

Recovery:

SERVER FAILURE
 ↓
RESTART
 ↓
LOAD LAST VALID CHECKPOINT
 ↓
REPLAY REQUIRED EVENTS
 ↓
VERIFY EXTERNAL STATE
 ↓
RESUME


---

3A.26 — State Projection Versioning

State reconstruction depends on versioned projection logic.

Projection {

    projection_id
    version

    input_event_schema
    output_state_schema
}

This allows historical state to remain reproducible even after the implementation evolves.


---

3A.27 — Orchestration Boundary

The Runtime provides execution machinery.

The Orchestration Controller decides:

Should work be decomposed?

Which capabilities are required?

Which methods are suitable?

Which resources should be used?

Which tasks can run in parallel?

Which tasks depend on others?

Is additional investigation required?

When is the global objective satisfied?

Possible execution forms:

deterministic operation
one resource
parallel specialists
competing analyses
hierarchical workers
sub-agent
no additional worker

No permanent swarm is assumed.


---

3A.28 — Stop Conditions

CNS must know when additional work is no longer justified.

Research may stop when:

evidence is sufficient
OR
expected value of additional information is too low
OR
uncertainty is irreducible
OR
budget exhausted
OR
deadline reached
OR
required resource unavailable
OR
decision is no longer actionable
OR
risk boundary reached


---

3A.29 — Evidence Lineage

Material conclusions must remain traceable.

SOURCE
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
ANALYSIS
 ↓
HYPOTHESIS
 ↓
SYNTHESIS
 ↓
DECISION

Repeated agreement between models does not automatically increase evidence quality.


---

3A.30 — Uncertainty Lineage

Uncertainty must propagate through the decision process.

Evidence uncertainty
       ↓
Analysis uncertainty
       ↓
Hypothesis uncertainty
       ↓
Decision uncertainty

The system must prevent:

weak evidence
 ↓
strong-looking conclusion

without a defensible basis.


---

3A.31 — Assumption Registry

Important decisions maintain explicit assumptions.

Assumption {

    assumption_id

    statement

    supporting_evidence[]

    dependent_decisions[]

    validity_conditions[]

    invalidation_triggers[]

    current_status

    last_verified
}

If an important assumption changes:

ASSUMPTION INVALIDATED
        ↓
DEPENDENT DECISIONS IDENTIFIED
        ↓
REASSESSMENT

This is how CNS keeps old decisions connected to changing reality.


---

3A.32 — Temporal Provenance

Important evidence records:

observed_at
available_at
effective_at
source_id
source_version

Therefore CNS can distinguish:

"What was true?"
from
"What was known at the time?"

This protects against historical information leakage.


---

3A.33 — Capability Gap Detection

If CNS requires a capability for which no suitable resource exists:

required capability
        ↓
no suitable resource
        ↓
CAPABILITY GAP

The gap becomes an explicit research/improvement target.

Capability Gap
 ↓
Mechanism research
 ↓
Prototype
 ↓
Benchmark
 ↓
Security test
 ↓
Register capability

The Runtime therefore supports future CNS expansion without hard-coding today's capability set.


---

3A.34 — Resource Health

Resources maintain observable health states:

HEALTHY
DEGRADED
UNAVAILABLE
QUARANTINED
RECOVERING
RETIRED

Health may consider:

failure rate
latency
schema errors
quality
drift
availability
resource pressure
recent outcomes

Resource self-reported confidence is not sufficient evidence of health.


---

3A.35 — Failure Classification

Failures are classified before recovery.

TRANSIENT
RESOURCE
DATA
LOGIC
AUTHORITY
RISK
SYSTEM
SECURITY
UNKNOWN

Recovery may include:

retry
fallback
reassign
replan
quarantine
reconcile
stop
human escalation

Unknown failures are not blindly retried indefinitely.


---

3A.36 — Security and Blast Radius

A module receives bounded:

authority
data access
resource access
execution scope

A defective module must not automatically be able to:

modify arbitrary state
execute arbitrary commands
access unrelated secrets
bypass risk controls
modify CNS constitution
approve its own deployment

This makes modularity safe.


---

3A.37 — Tool Execution Boundary

Every consequential tool call passes through:

REQUEST
 ↓
IDENTITY
 ↓
CAPABILITY
 ↓
RESOURCE
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
POLICY
 ↓
SCHEMA VALIDATION
 ↓
RISK CHECK
 ↓
PRE-EXECUTION
 ↓
EXECUTION
 ↓
RESULT VALIDATION
 ↓
PROVENANCE
 ↓
EVENT
 ↓
POST-EXECUTION

Financial execution additionally requires:

market state
risk state
portfolio state
execution constraints
broker/external state


---

3A.38 — Financial Safety Boundary

The live trading path remains deterministic and independently protected:

MARKET DATA
    ↓
FEATURES
    ↓
STRATEGY
    ↓
RISK
    ↓
ORDER VALIDATION
    ↓
EXECUTION

LLMs and agents may:

research
analyse
propose
challenge
explain
discover

but cannot bypass:

risk
authority
validation
execution controls

This remains a permanent Log 1 invariant.


---

3A.39 — Ecosystem Learning Hook

The Runtime records structured experience:

CONTEXT
 ↓
INTENT
 ↓
METHOD
 ↓
RESOURCE
 ↓
EXECUTION
 ↓
RESULT
 ↓
OUTCOME
 ↓
EXPECTED vs ACTUAL
 ↓
PERFORMANCE
 ↓
LEARNING

This allows CNS to eventually learn:

> Which methods, resources and combinations perform best under particular conditions.



The exact learning algorithms are deliberately not frozen here.


---

3A.40 — Replay

CNS must support reconstruction of previous execution:

EVENT HISTORY
+
PROJECTION VERSION
+
SCHEMA VERSION
+
PROVENANCE
        ↓
RECONSTRUCTED STATE

Replay must answer:

What happened?

What did CNS know?

Which evidence was available?

Which resource acted?

What assumptions existed?

What decision was made?

What was expected?

What actually happened?


---

3A.41 — Stable Interface / Replaceable Implementation

The core follows:

STABLE CONTRACT
      ↓
REPLACEABLE IMPLEMENTATION
      ↓
MEASUREMENT
      ↓
FAILURE / NEW EVIDENCE
      ↓
REPLACEMENT OR IMPROVEMENT
      ↓
SAME CONTRACT

CNS does not freeze uncertain technology choices into permanent architecture.


---

3A.42 — Runtime Interface

Conceptually:

trait CnsRuntime {

    register_module(...)
    unregister_module(...)

    register_capability(...)
    register_method(...)
    register_resource(...)

    create_intent(...)
    create_work(...)
    submit_task(...)

    emit_event(...)
    read_state(...)

    execute_command(...)

    checkpoint(...)
    resume(...)

    reconcile(...)
    cancel(...)

    replay(...)
    inspect(...)
}

The implementation behind these interfaces remains replaceable.


---

3A.43 — Explicit Non-Assumptions

3A does not assume:

one permanent brain
one permanent LLM
one agent framework
one memory implementation
one database
one orchestration algorithm
one ML architecture
one learning algorithm
one market representation
one cloud provider
one execution provider

These belong to later construction layers.


---

3A.44 — 3A Acceptance Requirements

3A is considered technically acceptable only when the implementation can demonstrate:

CORE-001
replace a module without redesigning consumers

CORE-002
recover from module failure

CORE-003
recover interrupted work

CORE-004
reconstruct state from durable history

CORE-005
replay historical execution

CORE-006
prevent unauthorized execution

CORE-007
prevent duplicate consequential commands

CORE-008
reconcile uncertain external outcomes

CORE-009
preserve evidence provenance

CORE-010
preserve temporal information boundaries

CORE-011
continue safely under non-critical resource failure

CORE-012
block operations when critical capability is unavailable

CORE-013
detect capability gaps

CORE-014
maintain bounded module blast radius

CORE-015
demonstrate trading-path independence from LLM availability


---

3A.45 — Final 3A Construction Principle

CNS is not being built as a collection of permanently fixed technologies.

It is being built as a stable ecosystem capable of replacing, testing, learning from and improving its own internal mechanisms.

The Runtime therefore provides:

CONTROL
STATE
EVENTS
WORK
CAPABILITIES
METHODS
RESOURCES
AUTHORITY
RECOVERY
PROVENANCE
REPLAY

while later CNS layers provide:

BRAIN
AGENTS
BOTS
ML
MEMORY
RESEARCH
VERIFICATION
DECISION
SELF-IMPROVEMENT
EXECUTION

The architecture must allow those higher layers to become dramatically more capable without requiring the foundation to be rebuilt.

Core principle

> Build the foundation strong enough that CNS can change what is inside it without losing what CNS is.



